WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:01.970
The NFL isn't concerned about you as a fan

00:00:01.970 --> 00:00:04.160
the NFL is concerned about opening up your
pocketbook

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:06.240
and pulling as much money out of it as they
possibly can.

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:08.960
The NFL is the most lucrative sports league
in the world

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:13.480
and many cities seem desperate to have a football
franchise to call their own.

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:15.700
But no city is more eager than Los Angeles

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:20.760
the country's second largest metropolis and
football desert since 1994.

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:23.400
But does it make sense to invest scarce public
dollars

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:27.030
on a sports operation that posts billions
in yearly profits

00:00:27.030 --> 00:00:30.380
and counts 18 of it's owners on Forbes billionaire
list?

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:35.460
In general about 70% of the cost of building
and maintaining NFL stadiums

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:37.120
has been payed for by taxpayers.

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:39.520
With city budgets being pushed to the brink,

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:43.440
is it time to demand that the NFL carry their
own financial weight?

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:46.140
There's a beauty to the game, I'd like to
see the in Lost Angeles.

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:49.800
I like what it does for a city when they feel
good when their team wins.

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:53.760
Or will places like Los Angeles continue to
push for a costly stadium

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:56.040
built on the taxpayer's dime.

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:10.570
The NFL generated over 9 billion in revenues
last year

00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:13.580
and many cities are hungry for a piece of
the action.

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:18.860
Often spending huge sums of taxpayer dollars
to attract the league to their localities.

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:21.660
I say in 'The King of Sports' the NFL
is good at two things:

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:25.940
Providing high-quality sports entertainment,
the games are, consistently terrific

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:27.120
and I'm hooked.

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:29.740
And the NFL is good at fleecing taxpayers.

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:34.039
It's about a billion dollars a year I'd calculate
public subsidies to NFL owners

00:01:34.039 --> 00:01:37.579
and this is a group that consist almost entirely
of billionaires

00:01:37.580 --> 00:01:40.700
and yet receiving significant public subsidies
every year.

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:43.840
Nowhere is this illustrated more than in Los
Angeles

00:01:43.850 --> 00:01:47.969
where the city's political and investor class
has been trying for nearly two decades

00:01:47.969 --> 00:01:50.280
to get a team back in the area.

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:51.640
And though study after study

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:56.100
has shown that sports stadiums don't increase
local economic activity,

00:01:56.100 --> 00:02:01.100
it hasn't stopped cash-strapped cities like
LA from approving billion dollar stadium plans

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:03.900
that would be largely funded with public dollars.

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:09.140
So is subsidizing a multi-billion dollar organization
worth the public cost?

00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:10.960
City councilman Tom Labonge thinks so

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:14.920
and is part of the effort to bring the NFL
back to Los Angeles.

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:22.680
My council district has 274,020 people, more
than Buffalo and just a little less than Cincinnati

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:28.300
So you could put a NFL in my district.

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:31.740
Labonge who spent part of his early career
with NFL Films

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:36.240
feels a personal stake in bringing the NFL
back to the city he loves.

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:39.500
Each season he writes the league and team
owners to make the case

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:42.140
for football in Los Angeles.

00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:45.360
I have a son who's 15 and he's never had a
home town team.

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:49.340
I said, you know, I just wanna reach out,
it was a simple reach out

00:02:49.340 --> 00:02:52.600
got a couple of responses it was good

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:54.760
but someone from the National
Football League has to say

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:56.280
'I wanna be in Los Angeles.'

00:02:56.280 --> 00:02:59.680
'I wanna be in the second largest market in
the country.'

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:00.940
But no one has.

00:03:00.940 --> 00:03:04.180
Does having a generation of fans without a
home town team

00:03:04.190 --> 00:03:07.910
justify spending loads of public money on
a stadium?

00:03:07.910 --> 00:03:11.150
Especially in a place like Los Angeles which
is chronically in debt

00:03:11.150 --> 00:03:14.480
and hampered by excessive regulations.

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:20.440
People would be, they'll see public money
going to what is a private enterprise

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:24.240
that is somewhat on the up.

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:27.700
They would like to see money go to develop
jobs that are current

00:03:27.710 --> 00:03:31.430
but you know there's only 8 regular season
games at home

00:03:31.430 --> 00:03:34.840
if you get the playoffs then you get a few
or pre-season games.

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:39.520
I would be cautious on spending heavy
public money on that.

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:43.420
Even Labonge realizes that financing a stadium
with taxpayer money

00:03:43.420 --> 00:03:46.700
may not be the most responsible act of city
government.

00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:52.360
Yet he and his fellow council members voted
unanimously for a 1.2 billion dollar stadium plan,

00:03:52.360 --> 00:03:53.980
known as Farmer's Field,

00:03:53.980 --> 00:03:58.100
that would be funded with nearly 350 million
in public bonds.

00:03:58.110 --> 00:04:01.930
The proposed LA deal pales in comparison to
the recent sweetheart deals

00:04:01.930 --> 00:04:04.040
cut by Atlanta and Minneapolis,

00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:09.160
Which gave the NFL over a billion dollars
of public subsidies to build new facilities.

00:04:09.160 --> 00:04:13.220
And because of how the deals are structured,
the league, not the cities,

00:04:13.220 --> 00:04:17.080
get the lion's share of the revenue derived
from the stadium and concessions.

00:04:17.080 --> 00:04:20.760
Making it difficult for taxpayers to recover
their investment.

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:24.090
Daniel Durbin, the director of the University
of Southern California's

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:27.480
Institute of Sports, Media and Society explains.

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:33.500
Well in theory the value that it brings to
your city financially, in theory,

00:04:33.500 --> 00:04:38.340
could be so high that it replaces the costs
of doing business there.

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:43.640
Although California has been more than happy
to wheel out money for all sorts of projects

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:47.070
like the bullet train, that we just keep putting
money into and putting money into

00:04:47.070 --> 00:04:49.560
and Lost Angeles is the same way.

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:53.980
The city government tends to put a lot of
money in projects that may or may not play out.

00:04:53.980 --> 00:04:59.220
Just the sheer land costs of a stadium are
horribly exorbitant in southern California,

00:04:59.220 --> 00:05:02.540
but beyond that you have the difficulty of
the environment in LA.

00:05:02.540 --> 00:05:07.440
In Los Angeles you don't have, we haven't
had an NFL team since 1995

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:09.040
so that's almost twenty years.

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:11.530
And you don't notice people really crying
about it a lot

00:05:11.530 --> 00:05:12.980
or worrying about it a lot.

00:05:12.980 --> 00:05:15.240
In Los Angeles we've got beaches, we've got
Hollywood,

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:17.260
we've got all sorts of, we've got Disneyland,

00:05:17.260 --> 00:05:19.620
we've got all sorts of things to do during
the winter.

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:24.100
We don't live and die by the NFL, so Los Angeles
really doesn't need the NFL.

00:05:24.100 --> 00:05:28.380
The Irony is that even if Los Angeles did
manage to build a stadium

00:05:28.380 --> 00:05:30.920
there's no reason to think the NFL may bite.

00:05:30.920 --> 00:05:33.120
Through it all the league has remained non-committal

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:34.100
telling Reason:

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:48.880
Their reluctance may not be a bad thing,

00:05:48.880 --> 00:05:53.620
thanks to the expansion of sports broadcast
and the NFL's byzantine blackout rules,

00:05:53.620 --> 00:05:58.320
having a franchise can make it hard for loyal
fans to even watch their home town team play.

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:01.760
If we have a team in Los Angeles and that
team doesn't sell out

00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:03.620
they can black it out in Los Angeles

00:06:03.620 --> 00:06:08.520
which means that you very often will lose
games as a fan.

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:10.800
And for fans there's no payback to that.

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:15.360
In the 80s and early 90s when Los Angeles
had two teams here that almost never sold out,

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:21.560
the Raiders and the Rams, the audience was
losing games consistently.

00:06:21.560 --> 00:06:25.280
If I can be a fan of the New England Patriots
in Los Angeles,

00:06:25.280 --> 00:06:27.940
if I can be a fan of the Buffalo Bills in
Los Angeles,

00:06:27.940 --> 00:06:31.260
and follow the team and certainly with online

00:06:31.270 --> 00:06:36.169
and with all of the cable broadcasts being
at least accessible to me

00:06:36.169 --> 00:06:40.980
I can watch any game that I want to if I have
the ingenuity and the money.

00:06:40.980 --> 00:06:44.420
If I can be a fan of those teams, what does
it pay me back

00:06:44.420 --> 00:06:46.840
to go to a local stadium in Los Angeles.

00:06:46.840 --> 00:06:50.600
So in Los Angeles, you know, if you look at
it as a fan,

00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:54.300
you're the one who's chasing, you know, if
you really want a NFL team here,

00:06:54.300 --> 00:06:56.360
you're always chasing that relationship.

00:06:56.360 --> 00:06:58.639
Because the NFL isn't concerned about you
as a fan

00:06:58.639 --> 00:07:00.840
the NFL is concerned about opening up your
pocketbook

00:07:00.840 --> 00:07:03.160
and pulling as much money out of it as they
possibly can.

00:07:03.160 --> 00:07:05.460
While local boosters like councilman Labonge

00:07:05.460 --> 00:07:08.820
may continue to dream of luring the NFL to
their city,

00:07:08.820 --> 00:07:11.660
it's becoming increasingly clear that not
having a team

00:07:11.660 --> 00:07:15.500
may be the best deal for taxpayers and fans
alike.

00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:18.160
Perhaps it's a model other cities should emulate.

